The swap is wrong - it assigns `c = a` then `a = c` (which is now `a`), so nothing actually swaps properly. Same issue in the second swap.

The algorithm should:
1. Sort the three sticks so we know which is the largest
2. The triangle inequality for positive area requires: a + b > c (where c is the largest)
3. If not satisfied, we need `c - (a + b - 1)` more minutes (to make a + b = c + 1)

Let me fix the swap logic:

```csharp
using System.Linq;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System;

namespace MyApp
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            int a = 0, b = 0, c = 0;
            var inp = Console.ReadLine().Split();
            a = Convert.ToInt32(inp[0]);
            b = Convert.ToInt32(inp[1]);
            c = Convert.ToInt32(inp[2]);

            // Sort so that c is the largest
            int[] arr = new int[] { a, b, c };
            Array.Sort(arr);
            a = arr[0];
            b = arr[1];
            c = arr[2];

            int ans = 0;
            if(c - (a + b - 1) > ans) {
              ans = c - (a + b - 1);
            }
            Console.WriteLine(ans);
        }
    }
}
```

This correctly sorts the array so `c` is the maximum, then computes the minimum increments needed.

