Looking at the bug, I need to understand what the code does vs. what it should do.

The problem: For each time value (1-9), count how many panels need to be pressed at that time. With two hands each pressing at most k panels, we can press at most 2*k panels at any given time. If any time value has more than 2*k panels, output "NO", otherwise "YES".

The buggy code:
```csharp
cnt[ch]++;
// ...
Write(cnt.Max() > 2 * k ? "NO" : "YES");
```

The issue: `cnt[ch]++` counts ALL characters including `.` (periods). The `cnt['.']` could be large and `cnt.Max()` would return the count of periods if there are many. We should only check counts for digit characters ('1' through '9').

The fix is to only count digit characters and check only those counts:

```csharp
foreach (char ch in string.Concat(ReadLines(4)))
    if (ch >= '1' && ch <= '9')
        cnt[ch]++;
```

This way, periods are ignored and we only check actual panel counts.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Globalization;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
