The issue is with `cin.eof()` - the EOF flag might be set during `cin >> c` when it fails, but the value of `c` at that point is undefined. The loop condition `while (!cin.eof())` checks EOF before reading, but after the last successful read, we read again, fail (EOF set), and then the loop condition fails - but we already incremented `len` incorrectly if the failed read modified `c`.

